<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fayzabad, Badakhshan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Faizabad (Afghanistan)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fayzabad is a town in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, the capital of Badakhshan province, on the Kokcha river in the foothills of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a>. From the 17th century it was the seat of the mirs who ruled Badakhshan, and it has long been the main market of the province, on routes towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains">Pamirs</a>, Chitral and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fayzabad-badakhshan.jpg" alt="Fayzabad, Badakhshan" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julian-G. Albert · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Feyzabad_in_Afghanistan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fayzabad stands on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokcha-river">Kokcha River</a>, near where the river leaves a gorge and before it reaches a large open plain, at an altitude of 1,254 metres. Historically it connected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and it is the main commercial and administrative centre of the Pamir region. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> trade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and cutlery. Barley, wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> are grown around it, and gold is panned and salt mined nearby. Before the 21st century the town was relatively isolated from the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> for lack of paved roads.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Until 1680 the town was called Jauz Gun because of the many walnut farms in the area (jauz means walnut). It was renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a>, roughly the abode of divine bounty, blessing and charity, when the robe of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> was delivered there. Tradition holds that Muhammad Shaykh Ziya and Shaykh Niyaz brought it after Wais Qaran had carried it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>. The city then replaced Munjan as the capital of Badakhshan. The relic was removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali">Ahmad Shah Durrani</a> in 1768, who established the Kirka Sharif there. Seven historical forts, several now in ruins, were built in and around the city to defend it and the road in and out.</p><p>In 1821 Mohammad Murad Beg destroyed the city and its inhabitants were removed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunduz">Kunduz</a>. After Emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdur-rahman-khan">Abdur Rahman Khan</a> annexed it in 1888 the town regained its former importance and became a considerable place of trade. In 1979 it became a base of Afghan mujahideen resisting the Soviet invasion, and Soviet forces took it in 1980 and made it one of their bases in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fayzabad is the capital of Badakhshan Province, with an estimated population of 85,477, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajiks">Tajiks</a>, with Pashtun, Pamiri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hazara">Hazara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> communities. Eleven languages are spoken there, among them Dari, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashto">Pashto</a>, Wakhi, Shughni and Uzbek. It is home to Badakhshan University and serves as the gateway to the Wakhan National Park in Wakhan District.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fayzabad, Badakhshan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 1657 a local dynasty of mirs of the Yarid family, claiming saintly descent, established independent rule from <b>Faizabad</b> on the Kokcha River.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-badakhshan">Principality of Badakhshan</a></li></ul></section>
